    "lawCode": "Add §264, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4401\n\nSPONSOR: WEBB\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to physician course-\nwork or training in nutrition\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require physicians to be provided with information about continuing\nmedical education coursework or training in nutrition.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the public health law by adding a new section 264.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew medical research indicates that nearly one in five deaths worldwide\nis associated with poor diet. Poor diet is the leading cause of mortal-\nity in the United States, leading to more than half a million deaths\nevery year. These deaths were primarily associated with diets short on\nfruits, vegetables, and fiber, and heavy in sugar, salt, and saturated\nfats. Additionally, the correlation between morbidity and severity of\nCOVID-19 infection and underlying conditions that can often be treated\nwith dietary interventions (e.g., heart disease, high blood pressure,\ntype 2 diabetes) has now been well-established.\n\nIn New York State, practicing physicians are not required to complete\nany continuing medical education (CME) on nutrition and diet, and 75% of\nmedical school curricula do not include the minimum number of hours in\nnutrition coursework recommended by the National Research Council. This\noften means conversations about the benefits of a balanced diet are\nnever broached between doctor and patient, even when patients seek\nsupport for diet-related illnesses or conditions.  This legislation\nrequires the New York State Department of Health to create, maintain and\nshare with physicians annually a resource library that includes informa-\ntion about continuing medical education and training opportunities\nregarding nutrition as a clinically proven intervention for diet-related\ndiseases as well as continuing education resources regarding racial\ndisparities in outcomes for diet-related diseases. The resource library\nshall be developed by the Department in consultation with organizations\nrepresentative of professions, institutions, and those with expertise in\nnutrition and diet-related illnesses. The department shall also conduct\nsurveys at the two, four and six years after year one of implementation\nto ascertain enrollment in nutrition CME courses and the efficacy of\nemail distribution of the resource library. This legislation is integral\nto addressing a gap in physician medical education and training and to\nhelping New Yorkers receive the best medical care possible.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
